            {
              "id": "b455a5a2-da36-37ad-ad1d-9bee821f2d4f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random numbers, which is a security vulnerability. This is because if an attacker can predict the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated, which can lead to various types of attacks, such as brute force attacks.\n\nIn the given code example, `srand(0)` is used to seed the random number generator. This is a static value, which means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated. This makes the random numbers predictable, which is a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. One way to do this is to use the current time, but this is still predictable to some extent. A better way is to use a value that is more difficult for an attacker to predict, such as the process ID or a value from a hardware random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix that uses the process ID to seed the random number generator:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    srand(getpid() ^ time(NULL));\n    // rest of the code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- ctime\n- unistd.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0e4494c4-01b1-3419-b33d-76f583b16f95",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow if the input is not properly validated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to perform proper input validation and error checking. For `atoi()`, a safer alternative is `strtol()`, which allows for error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()`. Here is an example of how to do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar *p;\nlong converted = strtol(argv[1], &p, 10);\nif (*p) {\n    // conversion failed because the input wasn't a number\n} else {\n    num_elements_per_proc = converted;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `cstdlib` (for `strtol()` function)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Buffer_overflow_attack)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b02b5015-e26c-335b-9502-e6be628eda86",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a cryptographic context because they do not provide sufficient randomness and can be easily predicted, leading to potential security risks.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the use of `rand()` function:\n\n```cpp\nrand_nums[i] = (rand() / (float) RAND_MAX);\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use functions provided by the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nrand_nums[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "b02b5015-e26c-335b-9502-e6be628eda86"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "80275ba6-9370-3c58-a8d6-636b91c2ce82",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value for seeding randomness can lead to predictable randomness, which is a security vulnerability. This is because the `srand()` function is often seeded with the current time (using `time(NULL)`), which only changes once per second. This means that if an attacker knows the time at which `srand()` was called, they can predict the output of `rand()`. Similarly, using a static value for seeding randomness will always produce the same sequence of numbers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a more unpredictable seed for `srand()`. A good source of randomness is often necessary to generate secure random numbers in a program. This can be achieved by using a high-quality random number generator that is designed for cryptographic purposes, such as `std::random_device`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `srand(time(NULL))` with `std::random_device` as shown below:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 mt(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<double> dist(1.0, 10.0);\n\nfor (int i=0; i<16; ++i)\n    std::cout << dist(mt) << \"\\n\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`\n- `<iostream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ee2c94fc-ae61-360d-bf33-f640444d8dd2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. One such function is `fprintf`.\n\nThe `fprintf` function can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. It can cause format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not a constant, or if it's composed dynamically, it can lead to buffer overflows if the output is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. These functions include the size of the buffer as an argument, which can prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fprintf`, use `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // Now buffer contains the formatted string.\n    // You can write it to file with fwrite or similar\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e3293958-be94-3732-a9ef-888de3b21ec8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a cryptographic context because they do not provide sufficient randomness and can be easily predicted, leading to potential security risks.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the use of `rand()` function:\n\n```cpp\nrand_nums[i] = (rand() / (float) RAND_MAX);\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use functions provided by the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nrand_nums[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "e3293958-be94-3732-a9ef-888de3b21ec8"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9330355c-f349-34d0-b5c0-1fe0c6a17ace",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards due to their potential misuse or abuse.\n\nIn the given code sink `argc > 1`, there is no direct use of a prohibited C function. However, `argc` is a parameter in the `main` function that represents the number of command-line arguments passed to the program. If not properly validated, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this potential vulnerability, you should always validate the command-line arguments (`argc` and `argv`) before using them. Ensure that the number of arguments (`argc`) is as expected and that the arguments themselves (`argv`) are valid and safe to use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how you can validate `argc` and `argv`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    if (argc != 2) {\n        std::cerr << \"Usage: \" << argv[0] << \" <input>\\n\";\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Now it's safe to use argv[1]\n    std::cout << \"Input: \" << argv[1] << \"\\n\";\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the program expects exactly one command-line argument (in addition to the program name itself). If the number of arguments is not as expected, the program prints a usage message and exits with a non-zero status code.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the following library:\n\n- iostream\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "df736ca2-38b6-3649-83bd-dd75a941aa56",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other vulnerabilities. These functions are often part of the C standard library and are included for backwards compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the case of `argc > 2`, this is not a vulnerability in itself, but it could potentially lead to vulnerabilities if the additional command line arguments are not properly handled. For example, if these arguments are used to construct file paths or SQL queries without proper sanitization, it could lead to path traversal or SQL injection vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions in your C++ code. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. For example, instead of using `strcpy()`, use `std::string`.\n\nIn the case of `argc > 2`, ensure that all command line arguments are properly sanitized before they are used. This could involve checking that file paths do not contain directory traversal sequences (e.g., `../`), or using parameterized queries for SQL.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf your program requires more than two command line arguments, you should handle them in a safe manner. Here is an example of how you might do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    if (argc > 2) {\n        std::vector<std::string> args(argv + 1, argv + argc);\n        // Now args contains all command line arguments as std::strings.\n        // You can process them in a safe manner.\n    } else {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: Too few arguments.\\n\";\n        return 1;\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis code uses the `std::vector` and `std::string` classes from the C++ standard library, which handle memory management automatically and are generally safer than their C counterparts.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThis code requires the following libraries:\n\n- iostream\n- vector\n- string\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4a5e8b0a-0c34-3fec-89f0-71e4f3badff1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential for misuse, leading to buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `argc != 3` is not a vulnerability sink. It's a condition that checks if the number of command-line arguments is not equal to 3. However, if the program uses dangerous C functions to handle command-line arguments, it could lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using dangerous C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. For example, use `std::string` and its methods for string manipulation instead of `strcpy`, `strcat`, etc. Use `std::cin` and `std::cout` for input/output instead of `scanf` and `printf`.\n\nAlways validate and sanitize input, especially if it comes from an untrusted source like command-line arguments or user input.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the complete source code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, here's a general advice:\n\nIf you're using `argv` array (which is usually the case when you're checking `argc`), make sure to validate the arguments before using them. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (argc != 3) {\n    std::cerr << \"Usage: \" << argv[0] << \" arg1 arg2\\n\";\n    return 1;\n}\n\nstd::string arg1 = argv[1];\nstd::string arg2 = argv[2];\n\n// Now use arg1 and arg2 safely\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet doesn't have any library dependencies. However, if you're using `std::string`, `std::cerr`, etc., you need to include the following headers:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <iostream>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            }
